Description: CVE-2026-80230 - openssl: no server cert is only okay if also not pinned
 When the OpenSSL backend received no server certificate, curl treated the
 connection as acceptable whenever peer/host verification was disabled, even
 if the application had configured a pinned public key
 (CURLOPT_PINNEDPUBLICKEY). This bypassed the pinning check entirely, allowing
 a man-in-the-middle to present no certificate and defeat pinning.
 .
 This backport adapts upstream commit 5267ed859d545534d0c21675a2b70af5a3b6e3ef
 to curl 8.14.1. The upstream fix introduces a new pinned() helper and the
 CURL_EASY_STR macro which are not present in this version, so the pinned key
 lookup is inlined at the "no server certificate" branch. A missing server
 certificate is now only acceptable when the peer is neither being verified
 nor has a pinned public key configured; otherwise it is treated as a
 verification failure (CURLE_PEER_FAILED_VERIFICATION).
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/5267ed859d545534d0c21675a2b70af5a3b6e3ef
Author: Daniel Stenberg <daniel@haxx.se>
Bug: https://github.com/curl/curl/issues/22660
Last-Update: 2026-09-09
